      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Critics argue that perpetual road expansion only induces more demand, resulting in the \"induced demand\" phenomenon where new capacity quickly fills up. Therefore, long-term environmental goals necessitate de-prioritizing new highway construction in favor of density and transit investment. However, some believe highways remain necessary as temporary infrastructure to support existing low-density development patterns while higher-capacity transit networks are built out.",
      "source": "target",
